  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong fit for the application-object-family pressure: new families can use new pcid values without reinterpreting old envelope bytes, while older peers retain exact bytes as quarantined evidence instead of silently trusting or destroying them. Explicit sig_pcid and accepted/quarantined/rejected outcomes materially improve migration safety and auditability, but durable pcid/profile discovery and inner payload-family binding remain outside this draft.",
    "strengths": [
      "New object families can be introduced by minting new pcid values without changing the meaning of old envelope bytes.",
      "Unknown-quarantine preserves exact bytes for later audit or replay while fail-closing semantic acceptance on unknown payload or signature semantics.",
      "Explicit sig_pcid plus tri-state receiver outcomes make verification rules and local evidence records clearer than either parent extreme."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The extensibility story is strongest at the envelope boundary; bare or inner application objects still need their own durable type/version binding rules.",
      "Long-term discovery and archival of pcid and sig_pcid specifications are assumed rather than specified.",
      "Mandatory signatures may be an awkward fit for some future application families that want unsigned or differently authenticated objects."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Quarantine-and-relay can create opaque backlog or spam pressure unless local storage and forwarding policy is defined.",
      "If pcid or signature-profile recovery fails over time, preserved bytes may remain permanently uninterpretable."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "How are pcid and sig_pcid artifacts discovered and preserved for 100-year recovery without a central registry?",
      "What relay and retention limits should apply to quarantined unknown families?",
      "Does the envelope need a standard unsigned or multi-signature profile path for some application families?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  },
